-- title:
--   Atkin–Lehner-type operator induces an automorphism of the q-expansion field
-- statement:
--   Let $N\le \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ be a subgroup containing $T=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$, let $p$ be a nonzero natural number, let $\gamma,\gamma'\in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic $0$ with a ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb C$, and let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L\subseteq L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ N)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the field generated over $L$ by the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb Q\to L$ of the subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated by the ratios $\hat p_f/\hat p_g$ of Laurent series attached to integral $q$-expansions (`IsIntegralQExp`, meaning the integral power series maps to `UpperHalfPlane.qExpansion 1`) of two modular forms $f,g$ of one weight for $N$ viewed in $GL_2(\mathbb R)$. Write $W_\gamma h:=\tau\mapsto (h\mid_k\gamma)(\mathrm{heckeDiagMatrix}\,p\cdot\tau)$, the slash by $\gamma$ followed by the action of $!!\,[p,0;0,1]$. Two hypotheses $hW$, $hW'$ state, for $\gamma$ and for $\gamma'$ respectively: for every weight $k$ and every modular form $f$ of weight $k$ for $N$ possessing an integral $q$-expansion, some nonzero integer multiple $D\cdot W_\gamma f$ is a finite linear combination, with coefficients in the range of $\iota$, of modular forms of weight $k$ for $N$ possessing integral $q$-expansions. Two further hypotheses give scalars $u,u':\mathbb Z\to\mathbb C$ with $W_{\gamma'}W_\gamma h=u(k)\,h$ and $W_\gamma W_{\gamma'}h=u'(k)\,h$ for every weight $k$ and every function $h$ on the upper half plane. The conclusion asserts the existence of an $L$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $K$ with four properties: first, every $x\in K$ admits a weight $k$ and modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ for $N$ with the Laurent series of $g$'s $q$-expansion nonzero, with $\iota$-image of $x$ equal to $\hat f/\hat g$, and such that nonzero integer multiples $D\cdot W_\gamma f$, $E\cdot W_\gamma g$, $D'\cdot W_{\gamma'}f$, $E'\cdot W_{\gamma'}g$ are again modular forms of weight $k$ for $N$; second, for any such presentation $x=\hat f/\hat g$ together with forms $\varphi=D\cdot W_\gamma f$ and $\psi=E\cdot W_\gamma g$ ($D,E\neq 0$), the coefficientwise $\iota$-image of $\sigma x$ equals $(E/D)\,\hat\varphi/\hat\psi$; third, the same formula computes $\sigma^{-1}x$ from data built with $\gamma'$ in place of $\gamma$; fourth, if an element of $K$ has underlying Laurent series the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) $=q^{-1}\cdot jNumQ$, then its image under $\sigma$ has underlying Laurent series the image of [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ p ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), that is $j(q^p)$.
--
--   This is a level-free form of the Atkin–Lehner mechanism: an operator $f\mapsto (f\mid_k\gamma)(p\tau)$ which preserves the property of having a rational (indeed integral) $q$-expansion induces, via $f/g\mapsto W_\gamma f/W_\gamma g$, an $L$-algebra automorphism of the $q$-expansion function field of the modular curve for $N$, acting on the modular invariant by $j(q)\mapsto j(q^p)$. It is the engine behind the Atkin–Lehner involution statements at level $Mp$, and is cited in the construction of automorphisms of the function fields of $X_1(Mp)$ and of $X_1(M)\times_{X_0(1)}X_0(p)$.
